.\n- For multi-step work, write a brief plan with a per-step verification check before executing, so you can loop independently instead of asking for confirmation each step.\n- Every changed line should trace back to the task. If it doesn't, drop it.\n\n## Naming\n\nEvery name you choose — files, directories, scripts, functions, variables, classes, commits,\nbranches, anything — uses precise professional vocabulary. Choose the word a 1972 IBM engineer\nwould write in a specification. No slang, no casual shorthand, no cute or clever names, no words\nborrowed from chat culture.\n\n- Match the surrounding convention first. Shell scripts in this repo are kebab-case\n  (`normalize-settings.sh`, `inject-soul.sh`); a new one follows suit. This rule governs word\n  choice, not the separator or the case style.\n- Name the action in the domain's own terms.\n  - Bad:  `push-dashboards.sh` for a script that deploys them\n  - Good: `deploy-dashboards.sh`\n  - Bad:  `do-stuff.sh`, `helper.py`, `utils2.ts`, `tmp-fix.nix`, `data2`, `newFunc`\n  - Good: `probe-host.sh`, `sort-json.sh`, `normalize-settings.sh`\n- No cute, jokey, or chat-culture names.\n  - Bad:  `yeet-old-gens.sh`, `nuke.sh`, `magic.py`, `doTheThing()`\n  - Good: `delete-old-generations.sh`, `collect-garbage.sh`, `renderInvoice()`\n- The examples above are illustrative, not exhaustive. The rule covers every identifier you\n  introduce.\n\n## CLI tools\n\nPrefer the Nix-managed tools below over slower or less precise defaults when they fit the task.\n\n| tool             | replaces                        | usage                                                                               |\n|------------------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `rg` (`ripgrep`) | `grep`                          | `rg \"pattern\"` - fast recursive text search                                         |\n| `fd`             | `find`                          | `fd -g \"*.nix\"` - fast file finder with sensible defaults                           |\n| `ast-grep`       | text-only code search           | `ast-grep run --pattern '$FUNC($$$)' --lang php src/` - AST-aware structural search |\n| `actionlint`     | ad hoc workflow review          | `actionlint` - lint GitHub Actions workflows                                        |\n| `zizmor`         | ad hoc workflow security review | `zizmor .github/workflows/` - audit GitHub Actions security posture                 |\n\nUse `ast-grep` for code patterns (avoids false positives from comments/strings). Fall back to `rg` for plain text, docs, and unsupported languages.\n\n## SCM\n\n- Never use `git reset --hard` or push without explicit permission.\n- If force-pushing is required, use `git push --force-with-lease --force-if-includes` (or just `git push --force-with-lease` if previous one is impossible, analyzing the root cause for fail first, and making sure these changes would not override remote branch updates)\n- Never merge PRs without explicit permission — always ask first, even if CI passes.\n- Prefer safe alternatives (`git revert`, new commits, temp branches).\n- If history rewrite seems necessary, explain and ask first.\n- Add `Co-Authored-By: chatgpt-codex-connector[bot] <199175422+chatgpt-codex-connector[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>` to commits by default, unless explicitly asked not to.\n\n## Using GitHub\n\nFor questions about GitHub, use the `gh` tool and `/octocat` skill.\nAlways run `gh` commands with escalated permissions; sandboxed `gh` calls fail in this environment because network access is restricted there.\nNever mention Codex in PR descriptions, PR comments, or issue comments unless asked explicitly.\nDo not include a \"Test plan\" section in PR descriptions.\n\n## Coding Standards\n\n### PHP Coding Standards\n\nWhen working with Laravel/PHP projects, always use the `php-guidelines-from-spatie` skill.\n\n## Reviewing external feedback\n\n- ALWAYS load the `superpowers:receiving-code-review` skill upon receiving review feedback (PR comments, agent reviews). The skill enforces technical rigor and verification over performative agreement.\n- When acting on PR review comments (from bots or humans), independently verify factual claims before applying fixes — especially version numbers, library existence, API behavior, and deprecation status.\n- If a claim falls outside your knowledge cutoff, use WebSearch or check local evidence (installed packages, lock files, docker images) before trusting it.\n- Use `pr-review-fetcher` agent to fetch Pull Review reviews and comments.\n\n## Production safety\n\n- Assume production impact unless stated otherwise.\n- Call out risk when touching auth, billing, data, APIs, or build systems.\n- Prefer small, reversible changes; avoid silent breaking behavior.\n\n## Self improvement\n\n- Continuously improve agent workflows.\n- When a repeated correction or better approach is found you're encouraged to codify your new found knowledge and learnings by modifying your section of `~/.c********S.md`.\n- You can modify `~/.c********S.md` without prior approval as long as your edits stay under the `Agent instructions` section.\n- If you utilize any of your codified instructions in future coding sessions call that out and let the user know that you performed the action because of that specific rule in this file.\n\n## Tool-specific memory\n\n- Actively think beyond the immediate task.\n- When using or working near a tool the user maintains:\n  - If you notice patterns, friction, missing features, risks, or improvement opportunities, jot them down.\n  - Do **not** interrupt the current task to implement speculative changes.\n- In plan mode, always use the `/ask` skill to read the code and the original transcript that generated it. Understanding intent will help you write a better plan.\n- Always start new conversation by loading `/using-superpowers` skill. Do this automatically without being asked.\n  - Ignore `superpowers:using-git-worktrees` skill.\n- Do **not** launch Bash / console commands in the background using `command &` syntax, you have options to run Bash and other tools in background explicitly.\n\n## External File Loading\n\nCRITICAL: When you encounter a file reference (e.g., @rules/general.md), use your Read tool to load it on a need-to-know basis. They're relevant to the SPECIFIC task at hand.\n\nInstructions:\n\n- Do NOT preemptively load all references - use lazy loading based on actual need\n- When loaded, treat content as mandatory instructions that override defaults\n- Follow references recursively when needed\n\n### Authoring CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md\n\n`@file` references in CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md are eagerly injected into agent context on every load by the Claude Code loader, regardless of whether the file is needed for the current task. The lazy-load instructions above govern in-conversation Read behavior; they do NOT override loader-level injection. Authoring choices matter:\n\n- Use `@file` ONLY for small, always-relevant docs (rule of thumb: ≤ ~50 lines and you'd want the content in every conversation in that directory).\n- Use plain markdown links `[file](file)` for everything larger or situational — agents will Read on demand.\n- Authoring heuristic: if you would not paste the file's full contents into every conversation that triggers this CLAUDE.md, do not use `@`.\n- This applies to both `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` (and `.claude.local.md`), and to symlinks between them.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Agent instructions - llame\n\nllame is a personal-first **meta-harness**: it owns Chat/Run identity, lifecycle,\nprovenance, and isolation; peer coding agents (ACP, A2A, and similar) stay\nexecutor adapters. See [VISION.md](VISION.md).\n\n[SPEC.md](SPEC.md) indexes cross-cutting architecture; OpenSpec owns capability\nbehavior. `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` link here.\n\n@README.md\n\n@CONTRIBUTING.md\n\n@CODING_STANDARDS.md\n\n@docs/research/harnesses/index.md\n\n@CLAU********l.md\n\n`CLAU********l.md` is optional and gitignored; tracked instructions must not\ndepend on it.\n\n## Documentation authority\n\n- [REVI********E.md](REVI********E.md): reviewer judgment.\n- [VISION.md](VISION.md), [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md): direction and unshipped work.\n- [SPEC.md](SPEC.md), [`openspec/specs`](openspec/specs): architecture and\n  shipped behavior. [`openspec/changes`](openspec/changes) owns proposals and\n  archives.\n- [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md): shipped chronology.\n- [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md): UI language.\n- [docs/testing.md](docs/testing.md), [docs/scaling.md](docs/scaling.md): test\n  placement and runtime topology.\n- [`docs/research`](docs/research): noncanonical evidence.\n- [docs/research/harnesses/index.md](docs/research/harnesses/index.md):\n  prior-art index for peer harnesses and protocols.\n\nPut commands and traps in the closest `AGENTS.md`, capability behavior in\nOpenSpec, architecture in `SPEC.md`, and operator procedures in `docs/`. The\nspec wins any disagreement.\n\n## Repository\n\npnpm 11 + Turborepo; TypeScript throughout; Node >=22.19 (`.node-version` pins\n22.23.1).\n\n| Path                         | Owner                                                     |\n| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `apps/api`                   | API, services, database, co-located and dedicated workers |\n| `apps/web`                   | thin Next.js client; no database                          |\n| `apps/storybook`             | Storybook runtime and browser tests                       |\n| `packages/ui`                | shared shadcn components and stories                      |\n| `packages/config-typescript` | shared tsconfig presets                                   |\n\n## Root commands\n\n```bash\npnpm install\npnpm dev\npnpm lint\npnpm format\npnpm test:mutation\npnpm test:mutation:check\npnpm test:mutation:dry\npnpm test:e2e\npnpm test:e2e:ui\npnpm test:e2e:headed\npnpm test:e2e:debug\npnpm test:e2e:report\n```\n\nBuild workspaces sequentially. Use `pnpm --filter <workspace> build`; if a full\nbuild is required, use `pnpm exec turbo run build --concurrency=1`. Never run\nthe unbounded root `pnpm build`.\n\nPlaywright owns its Postgres, migrations, API, model/MCP fixtures, and\nproduction web build unless `POSTGRES_URL` points at an external database;\nthat database is neither started, reset, nor migrated. Stop services using its default ports\n`4300/4301/55433/4302/4303/4304` and any Next process using `apps/web` before\nE2E. Authenticated tests use `e2e/support/fixtures.ts`; destructive session\ntests use `freshAccount`.\n\n## Local database\n\nCopy `apps/api/.env.example` to `apps/api/.env.local`, then:\n\n```bash\npnpm db:up\npnpm db:reset\npnpm db:migrate\npnpm db:provision-rls\npnpm db:studio\npnpm db:psql\npnpm db:logs\n```\n\n`apps/api/src/db` owns the schema. See its\n[AGENTS.md](apps/api/src/db/AGENTS.md) for migrations, RLS, and provisioning.\n\n## Pre-launch evolution\n\nUntil the first production deployment:\n\n- Remove obsolete internal code, schemas, APIs, aliases, and transitional paths\n  directly. Do not add compatibility shims, dual reads/writes, or preservation\n  backfills unless requested.\n- Update internal callers and tests atomically.\n- Test databases are disposable; Leo's running instance is not. Agree before a\n  change discards its chats.\n- Migration history is replaceable only with a coordinated baseline change.\n  Never change statements in an already-applied migration without resetting\n  every database that ran it; comment-only edits are safe.\n\nThese invariants remain: tenant tables use enabled and forced RLS with trusted\nidentity and fail closed; migrations remain transactional, idempotent, and\ndeterministic; secrets never reach logs, errors, model context, or owner output.\n\n## Repository conventions\n\n- Modified cyclomatic complexity stays `< 25`. Extract only at a responsibility\n  boundary; no metric gaming or inline disables.\n- Use Drizzle for DB access and generate migrations by default. A manual\n  security/data step follows the SQL-comment contract in\n  [apps/api/src/db/AGENTS.md](apps/api/src/db/AGENTS.md).\n- Use conventional commits.\n- Root `.oxlintrc.json` declares each rule once. Workspace configs only extend,\n  scope environments, ignore generated/vendor files, or record narrow reviewed\n  exceptions. Fix code rather than weakening a repository rule.\n- `pnpm lint` owns repository rules. Chained assertions, including\n  `as unknown as T`, are banned; narrow or validate the boundary.\n- Product Markdown is part of `pnpm lint` and available directly through\n  `pnpm lint:markdown`; lint commands reject unused disables.\n- Follow [docs/testing.md](docs/testing.md). DB suites never skip silently.\n- UI uses [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md), shared primitives, and semantic tokens.\n- Preserve `messages.parts` and stored order. Only declared display-only parts\n  stay outside model history; compaction replaces only its explicit prefix.\n  New replay transforms or omissions require a spec.\n\n## Storybook MCP\n\nWhen connected, Storybook tools are authoritative for props and stories. Read\nstory instructions before editing. After a rendered UI change, run story tests\nand return preview URLs. For shared files without their own story, use consumer\nstories.\n\n## Security\n\n- Identity comes only from authentication, never caller-controlled scope.\n- Enforce tenant isolation in the datastore and fail closed without identity.\n- Omit or gate any reachable surface that cannot yet be secured.\n- Data/auth/tenancy changes state threats up front and ship a negative isolation\n  test.\n- Never expose credentials, resolved secret values, or tokens. Keep\n  server-resolved host paths private; command-produced host paths may appear\n  in output from explicitly allowlisted host `bash`, under the\n  [bash execution contract](openspec/specs/bash-execution/spec.md).\n- Stop for a decision if a change may weaken isolation.\n\n## Current runtime traps\n\n- Every Chat Run uses pg-boss. HTTP enqueues and streams events;\n  `RunExecutionService` stays transport-neutral.\n\n</INSTRUCTIONS>\n<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/file-based-tool-prompt-templates</cwd>\n  <shell>zsh</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-09-12</current_date>\n  <timezone>Europe/Madrid</timezone>\n  <filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/file-based-tool-prompt-templates</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"managed\"><file_system type=\"restricted\"><entry access=\"read\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><path>/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/file-based-tool-prompt-templates</path></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/file-based-tool-prompt-templates/.git</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/file-based-tool-prompt-templates/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/file-based-tool-prompt-templates/.codex</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>\n</environment_context>",
